==============================================================================
3. SIMULATING A REAL NETWORK PARTITION
==============================================================================
  Partition: {N1,N2} (old leader's side, MINORITY) <-X-> {N3,N4,N5} (MAJORITY)
  (every node's outbound/inbound traffic to the other side is now silently dropped)

  Old leader N1, still believing it's leader, tries a write into the minority side:
  SET z=stuck (sent to old leader N1) -> {'ok': False, 'error': 'timeout_not_committed', 'index': 5}
  (it can't reach a majority to commit -- this call BLOCKS until COMMIT_WAIT_S, then times out)

  Meanwhile, the MAJORITY side (N3,N4,N5) elected its OWN new leader:
  New leader: N3, term 2  (strictly greater than old leader's term 1)

  Client writes now go through the NEW leader, and DO commit (majority is reachable):
  SET z=committed_by_new_leader -> {'ok': True, 'index': 6, 'term': 2}
  SET x=99 -> {'ok': True, 'index': 7, 'term': 2}

  Full cluster status DURING the partition (two leaders exist -- but only one can commit):
  N1: leader    term=1   leader=N1 commit=4   kv={'x': '3', 'y': '2'} blocked=[3, 4, 5]
  N2: follower  term=1   leader=N1 commit=4   kv={'x': '3', 'y': '2'} blocked=[3, 4, 5]
  N3: leader    term=2   leader=N3 commit=7   kv={'x': '99', 'y': '2', 'z': 'committed_by_new_leader'} blocked=[1, 2]
  N4: follower  term=2   leader=N3 commit=6   kv={'x': '3', 'y': '2', 'z': 'committed_by_new_leader'} blocked=[1, 2]
  N5: follower  term=2   leader=N3 commit=6   kv={'x': '3', 'y': '2', 'z': 'committed_by_new_leader'} blocked=[1, 2]

